---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import LessonIllustration from "../../components/LessonIllustration.astro";
import RegisterStructureDemo from "../../components/RegisterStructureDemo.astro";
import StructureFacts from "../../components/StructureFacts.astro";
import TermCallout from "../../components/TermCallout.astro";
import { registerCollectionTerm } from "../../lib/atlas/standalone-terms";
import "../../styles/set-lesson.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="RegisterCollection"
  description="The ranger retains sequenced trail-status versions and chooses an atomic or latest read."
  territory="Structures"
  sheetTitle="RegisterCollection"
>
  <article class="register-lesson">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <p class="territory-label">Structures</p>
      <h1>RegisterCollection</h1>
      <p class="structure-summary">
        Alice reports the trail open while Bob reports it closed. Neither has
        seen the other's note. The ranger records Alice's report as number 1
        and Bob's as number 2, then keeps both. Carol can ask for the atomic
        winner or the latest report.
      </p>
      <LessonIllustration id="structures-register-collection" />
    </header>

    <StructureFacts
      structure="RegisterCollection"
      family="DDS"
      model="Sequenced register collection"
      updates="Non-optimistic writes to named cells"
      merge="Retain sequenced versions; read as atomic or latest."
      delivery="One server-ordered operation stream"
      bestFor="Named cells that need more than one server-ordered read policy"
      cost="Retained competing values and sequence numbers"
    />

    <section aria-labelledby="versions-title">
      <h2 id="versions-title">The station keeps both sequenced versions</h2>
      <p>
        A <dfn><a href="/glossary/#registercollection">RegisterCollection</a></dfn>
        {" "}stores named registers. Writes stay hidden until the sequencer
        accepts them. The collection retains competing versions so an atomic
        read and a latest read can return different answers.
      </p>
      <TermCallout {...registerCollectionTerm} />
      <p>
        Alice and Bob each submit a report before either has seen a sequenced
        write. They both put <code>0</code> on their slips as the last sequence
        number they saw. The ranger then assigns Alice's report <code>1</code>
        {" "}and Bob's <code>2</code>. Bob's larger number does not mean he had
        seen Alice's report when he wrote his own.
      </p>
      <figure class="sequence-example">
        <table aria-label="RegisterCollection read policies">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Report</th>
              <th scope="col">Last sequence seen at submit</th>
              <th scope="col">Assigned sequence</th>
              <th scope="col">Atomic read</th>
              <th scope="col">Latest read</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Alice: Trail open</th><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>Trail open</td><td>Trail open</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Bob: Trail closed</th><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>Trail open</td><td>Trail closed</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Carol: Inspect bridge</th><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>Inspect bridge</td><td>Inspect bridge</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <figcaption>Carol's write is a later example, after the two-report race in the demo.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The ranger changes the atomic value only when a writer's last-seen
        number is at least the current atomic winner's sequence number.
        Bob's reference of <code>0</code> is less than the atomic winner's
        sequence number, <code>1</code>. The ranger keeps Alice as the atomic
        winner and Bob as the latest version. After Carol has seen sequence
        <code>2</code>, she writes <code>Inspect bridge</code>. Her reference
        of <code>2</code> lets her replace Alice's atomic value. The ranger
        assigns her report <code>3</code> and removes the older versions she
        saw, so both reads return her report.
      </p>
    </section>

    <RegisterStructureDemo
      kind="register-collection"
      title="Read the atomic winner or the latest version"
      description="Alice and Bob submit from the same reference point. The ranger sequences both, retains both, and exposes two read policies."
      rule="Atomic changes when a writer has seen its current winner; latest uses the greatest sequence number."
      raceLabel="Race the two station submissions"
    />

    <section aria-labelledby="choice-title">
      <h2 id="choice-title">The reader must choose a policy</h2>
      <p>
        In the demo's two-report race, an atomic read returns Alice's report.
        A latest read returns Bob's later report. Carol's later write shows
        that the atomic winner can change. Neither policy merges the reports
        or exposes causal siblings like an MvRegister.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a sequenced distributed data structure, not a CRDT. If the
        sequencer is unavailable, new writes cannot receive the ordering
        evidence that both read policies require.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="source-title">
      <h2 id="source-title">Sources and implementation</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Sources:</strong> Watershed's{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/blob/4a8739323ee491f353fcaa8ccfb0488419c1cd43/src/watershed/register_collection_kernel.gleam">RegisterCollection kernel</a>
        {" "}defines sequenced writes and atomic and latest read policies.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Implementation:</strong> The demo calls the kernel through the Gleam toolkit
        to sequence three clients' writes, apply remote changes, and inspect versions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <nav class="structure-next" aria-label="Continue through register structures">
      <a href="/structures/multi-value-register/">Back to MvRegister</a>
      <a href="/structures/registers/">Browse register family</a>
      <a href="/structures/maps/">Continue to maps</a>
    </nav>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .sequence-example {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .sequence-example table {
    min-width: 42rem;
  }
</style>
